{{!
    @template availability_proctor/launch

    The proctored launch page: hosts the SDK and embeds the quiz attempt.

    Context variables required for this template:
    * quizname - Name of the quiz, for the heading.
    * starturl - Core start-attempt URL, loaded into the iframe on examStarted.
    * tokenurl - Endpoint returning serverOrigin, integrationName and jwt.
    * eventurl - Endpoint recording SDK session events.
    * backurl - Quiz view page, for the abnormal-exit route.
    * modulesrc - This plugin's ES module.
    * sdksrc - The remotely served SDK module URL, passed to the module rather than
      hardcoded a second time there.

    Example context (json):
    {
        "quizname": "Final exam",
        "starturl": "https://moodle.example.com/mod/quiz/startattempt.php?cmid=7&sesskey=abc",
        "tokenurl": "https://moodle.example.com/availability/condition/proctor/token.php?sessionid=42&sesskey=abc",
        "eventurl": "https://moodle.example.com/availability/condition/proctor/event.php?sessionid=42&sesskey=abc",
        "backurl": "https://moodle.example.com/mod/quiz/view.php?id=7",
        "modulesrc": "https://moodle.example.com/availability/condition/proctor/js/proctor.js",
        "sdksrc": "https://sdk.web.proctor.constructor.app/proctoring.js"
    }
}}
{{!
    The URLs and the strings JavaScript needs are passed as data attributes
    rather than built in the module, so that no UI text is hardcoded in
    JavaScript and the module needs no separate string-loading round trip.
}}
<div id="proctor-sdk-app"
     data-starturl="{{starturl}}"
     data-tokenurl="{{tokenurl}}"
     data-eventurl="{{eventurl}}"
     data-backurl="{{backurl}}"
     data-sdksrc="{{{sdksrc}}}"
     data-errorlead="{{#str}} launch_error_lead, availability_proctor {{/str}}"
     data-errortechnical="{{#str}} launch_error_technical, availability_proctor {{/str}}"
     data-errorserver="{{#str}} launch_error_server, availability_proctor {{/str}}"
     data-timeoutmessage="{{#str}} launch_timeout, availability_proctor {{/str}}"
     data-abandonedmessage="{{#str}} error_abandoned, availability_proctor {{/str}}"
     data-requirement-browser="{{#str}} launch_requirement_browser, availability_proctor {{/str}}"
     data-requirement-camera="{{#str}} launch_requirement_camera, availability_proctor {{/str}}">

    <h1 class="sr-only accesshide">{{quizname}}</h1>

    {{!
        No confirmation here. Core already asks: the quiz view page shows its own
        "Start attempt" dialog with the time limit before it sends the student to
        startattempt.php, and this page is reached through that. A second prompt
        would be the same question twice.

        So proctoring starts on load, and this block is only what the student sees
        for the moment before the SDK paints over it.

        The user gesture browsers require for screen capture comes from the SDK's own
        preparation steps, which have their own buttons, rather than from a button
        here.

        Hidden with JavaScript off, through the "body:not(.jsenabled)" rule in
        styles.css: nothing is starting in that case, and leaving this on screen
        put "Starting proctoring" directly above the <noscript> notice saying the
        exam cannot start, which is two contradictory messages at once.
    }}
    <div id="proctor-sdk-intro" class="proctor-sdk-intro" aria-busy="true">
        <div class="proctor-sdk-intro-card">
            <p class="proctor-sdk-brand">{{#str}} pluginname, availability_proctor {{/str}}</p>
            <h2 class="proctor-sdk-quiz-name">{{quizname}}</h2>

            <div class="proctor-sdk-loading" role="status" aria-live="polite">
                <div class="proctor-sdk-spinner" aria-hidden="true">
                    {{> core/loading }}
                </div>
                <div>
                    <p class="proctor-sdk-status-title">
                        {{#str}} launch_preparing, availability_proctor {{/str}}
                    </p>
                    <p class="proctor-sdk-status-detail">
                        {{#str}} launch_starting, availability_proctor {{/str}}
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="proctor-sdk-stay-note">
                {{#str}} launch_requirement_stay, availability_proctor {{/str}}
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    {{!
        Without JavaScript there is no SDK, so there is no session and the access
        rule serves no questions. Saying so plainly beats leaving a dead button:
        the enforcement is server-side, so this notice is information, not a check.
    }}
    <noscript>
        <div class="alert alert-danger m-4">
            {{#str}} error_nojavascript, availability_proctor {{/str}}
        </div>
    </noscript>

    {{! Failures from the SDK or the token endpoint land here. }}
    <div id="proctor-sdk-error" class="alert alert-danger m-4 proctor-sdk-hidden" role="alert" aria-live="assertive"
         tabindex="-1">
        <p id="proctor-sdk-error-lead" class="mb-2"></p>
        <ul id="proctor-sdk-error-requirements" class="mb-0"></ul>
        <details id="proctor-sdk-error-detail" class="proctor-sdk-error-detail proctor-sdk-hidden">
            <summary></summary>
            <pre></pre>
        </details>
    </div>

    {{! Shown with the error, because a failed start needs a way out of it. }}
    <div id="proctor-sdk-recover" class="m-4 proctor-sdk-hidden">
        <button type="button" id="proctor-sdk-retry" class="btn btn-primary">
            {{#str}} launch_retry, availability_proctor {{/str}}
        </button>
        <a href="{{backurl}}" class="btn btn-secondary">
            {{#str}} backtoquiz, availability_proctor {{/str}}
        </a>
    </div>

{{!
    No CSS here for the SDK's own controls, on purpose.

    Overriding them was tried twice and both attempts were worse than the problem:
    reverting buttons stripped the SDK's own styling down to browser defaults, and
    forcing checkbox appearance only papered over the real cause.

    The real cause was ordering. The SDK injects its stylesheet when init() runs, and
    CSS of equal specificity is decided by document order, so starting the SDK before
    Moodle had finished loading its own stylesheets let Moodle's land last and flatten
    the SDK's controls. proctor.js waits for the load event instead, which is what a
    click used to guarantee for free. styles.css above is scoped to .proctor-sdk-*
    classes only, for the same reason.
}}

    {{! Shown while the exam runs, after examFinished stops the recording. }}
    <div id="proctor-sdk-tail" class="alert alert-warning proctor-sdk-tail proctor-sdk-hidden" role="status">
        {{#str}} notice_recordingstopped, availability_proctor {{/str}}
    </div>

    {{!
        The quiz attempt. src is set from JavaScript on examStarted rather than
        here, so the questions are never fetched before the session is live.

        The iframe works under either setting of allowframembedding, so no site
        configuration is needed. Off -- the default -- makes Moodle send
        X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN, which still permits a page to embed itself;
        on makes it send no such header at all. What does need re-verifying is a
        reverse proxy that rewrites the header, since that is outside Moodle's
        control.
    }}
    <iframe id="proctor-sdk-frame"
            title="{{quizname}}"
            class="proctor-sdk-frame proctor-sdk-hidden"
            allow="fullscreen">
    </iframe>
</div>

{{!
    A plain ES module, loaded directly rather than through Moodle's AMD pipeline,
    because the SDK is a remote ES module that RequireJS cannot load. This is the
    one place in the plugin where that matters.
}}
<script type="module">
    import {start} from '{{{modulesrc}}}';
    start(document.getElementById('proctor-sdk-app'));
</script>
